let __v_3893 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"nurse\",\"nursing\"]},\"Nursing wants five GCSEs at grade 4 or above, including English, maths and a science, and then a nursing degree, a nursing associate apprenticeship or a degree apprenticeship at a trust. Most universities also ask for A-levels or a BTEC in a science or health subject, and care experience - a care home, a hospice, a hospital volunteer - is what separates people with the same grades. Check the entry requirements for each course, because they differ.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"midwife\",\"midwifery\"]},\"Midwifery asks for the same five GCSEs at grade 4 or above, English, maths and a science among them, and then a midwifery degree or one of the new midwifery apprenticeships. It is one of the most competitive courses there is, so care or maternity experience and a week spent shadowing a midwife are worth more than an extra A-level.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"doctor\",\"medicine\",\"medical school\",\"surgeon\"]},\"Medicine wants strong GCSEs, usually 5s and 6s including maths, English and science, then A-level chemistry plus one more science or maths, because nearly every medical school requires chemistry. Then it is a five-year degree, the UCAT test in the summer before you apply, and work experience or volunteering in a care setting; the interview is about what you saw there.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"teacher\",\"teaching\",\"primary school teacher\",\"secondary school teacher\"]},\"Teaching wants GCSEs at grade 4 or above in English and maths, plus a science for primary, then a degree in a subject schools teach, then a PGCE or a school-based training place - or a teaching apprenticeship, where you are paid while you train. Primary teaching takes any degree but checks your curriculum subjects, and secondary wants the subject you will teach.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"vet\",\"veterinary\",\"vet school\"]},\"Veterinary medicine wants A-level chemistry and usually biology, grades A or A*, and weeks of work experience with animals - a farm, a stable, a vet practice - before you apply. The degree is five years and hard to get into, so apply to two or three vet schools and have a second choice you would genuinely take.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"lawyer\",\"law\",\"solicitor\",\"barrister\",\"law degree\"]},\"Law takes no particular A-levels: grades and a degree matter, and any subject is fine, so pick what you will do well in. The law degree (or any degree plus the conversion course) is followed by the SQE exams for solicitors or the bar course for barristers, and the thing that gets you a training place is experience - a law centre, a solicitor's office, mooting at university.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"engineer\",\"engineering\",\"mechanical engineer\",\"civil engineer\",\"electrical engineer\"]},\"Engineering wants A-level maths and physics, then an accredited MEng or BEng degree, and the accreditation is the part to check because it is what leads to chartership. The other route is a degree apprenticeship with an employer, which pays from day one and ends with the same qualification, or a technician apprenticeship if you would rather start with your hands.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"accountant\",\"accountancy\",\"accounting\",\"chartered accountant\"]},\"Accountancy asks for no fixed subjects, though maths and a numerical subject help, and there are three ways in: A-levels then an AAT or ACCA/ACA training contract, a degree then the same exams, or an accountancy degree apprenticeship where the employer pays for the exams. The exams are the qualification either way, so the route with the least debt is usually the apprenticeship.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"paramedic\",\"paramedic science\"]},\"Paramedic science wants a science A-level or an access course, and usually GCSEs at grade 4 or above in English, maths and science, then a three-year paramedic science degree - or the technician route inside an ambulance service, which pays while you train. Get a first aid certificate and some time in a care or St John Ambulance role first, because the courses and the interviews both ask what you have seen.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"electrician\",\"plumber\",\"plumbing\",\"mechanic\",\"chef\",\"carpenter\",\"bricklayer\",\"joiner\",\"gas engineer\"]},\"The trades ask for no GCSEs to start, though English and maths help with the theory and employers usually want 4s in them. The way in is an apprenticeship - level 2 then level 3 with an NVQ - or a college course alongside finding an employer yourself, and for gas, electrics and heating there is a registration scheme at the end that you cannot skip. Ring local firms and ask for a trial week: that is how most people get taken on.\"]}]}")?;
